{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.5,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "polarity",
      "components": ["J7", "J8"],
      "pins": ["J7.1", "J7.2", "J8.1", "J8.2"],
      "nets": ["+12V", "GND", "FAN_0_TACH", "FAN_1_TACH"],
      "observed": "On the Power sheet the two 4-pin fan headers are wired with the 12 V feed on the top (first) pin: the +12V symbol/node (same rail as the barrel jack, D7 and U5 IN1) is wired down into pin 1 of J7 and J8 (Conn_01x04_Pin), while pin 2 of each connector is wired to the GND symbol below the headers. Pin 3 of J7 carries FAN_0_TACH and pin 3 of J8 carries FAN_1_TACH; pin 4 of each is left no-connect.",
      "expected": "The specification states the fan-header 12 V pins are pin 2 of each header ('the two fan-header 12 V pins (J7/J8 pin 2)'), i.e. pin 1 = GND, pin 2 = +12 V, pin 3 = tach, pin 4 = NC — the standard 4-pin fan pinout. If the +12 V feed is on pin 1, then pin 2 is ground and the two supply pins of the header are effectively reversed.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "and the two fan-header 12 V pins (`J7`/`J8` pin 2)"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Power sheet: +12V symbol wired to first pin of J7/J8 (Conn_01x04_Pin); GND symbol wired to second pin of J7/J8; FAN_0_TACH on J7 pin 3, FAN_1_TACH on J8 pin 3"}
      ],
      "impact": "A fan plugged into either header using the standard pinout receives reversed supply polarity (12 V applied to its ground lead, ground on its +12 V lead). The fans will not spin, no tachometer edges will be produced, and the firmware (NUM_FANS = 2, which raises a run fault if a fan stalls) will abort oven runs with a fan fault.",
      "confidence": 0.55
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The board is otherwise consistent with the specification (power mux, buck, thermocouple channels, heater switch and ESP32 pin usage all trace as described), but the two fan headers J7/J8 appear to have their supply pins reversed: the +12 V rail is wired to pin 1 and ground to pin 2, whereas the specification states the fan-header 12 V pins are pin 2. This would reverse the supply polarity seen by any fan wired per the standard 4-pin pinout, so the fans would not run and the firmware would raise a stall fault."
}

